ctional CTO Asia-Pacific"], "faq": [["Is AI used in hiring high-impact under Korea's AI Basic Act?", "Commentary on the Act describes high-impact AI as including uses in areas such as hiring and lending, which bring duties around risk management, transparency and human oversight. Check the Act and its Enforcement Decree for the exact scope."], ["When did the AI Basic Act take effect?", "South Korea's AI Basic Act took effect on 22 January 2026."], ["What evidence should a hiring platform keep for AI decisions?", "For each application: which model and version ran, what it was given, what it produced, what the reviewer saw and decided, and the evaluation results for that model version. Tracing AI calls makes this a by-product of normal operation."]], "related": ["/ai-architect/", "/regions/", "/fractional-cto/"], "citations": [{"title": "South Korea AI Basic Act", "url": "https://www.trade.gov/market-intelligence/south-korea-ai-basic-act", "publisher": "International Trade Administration"}, {"title": "South Korea's AI Basic Act: overview and key takeaways", "url": "https://www.cooley.com/news/insight/2026/2026-01-27-south-koreas-ai-basic-act-overview-and-key-takeaways", "publisher": "Cooley", "date": "27 Jan 2026"}, {"title": "AI Watch: global regulatory tracker, South Korea", "url": "https://www.whitecase.com/insight-our-thinking/ai-watch-global-regulatory-tracker-south-korea", "publisher": "White & Case"}, {"title": "South Korea's AI Basic Act: seeking balance between industry innovation and social risk", "url": "https://www.stimson.org/2026/south-koreas-ai-basic-act-seeking-balance-between-industry-innovation-and-social-risk/", "publisher": "Stimson Center", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "Inside the LLM Call: GenAI Observability with OpenTelemetry", "url": "https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/genai-observability/", "publisher": "OpenTelemetry blog (James Newton-King)", "date": "14 May 2026"}]}-->
<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A South Korean recruitment platform served employers and job seekers. It used machine learning to match candidates to roles and, more recently, a language model to summarise applications and draft screening notes for recruiters. Employers liked the speed. Nobody had written down which steps used AI, how they were tested, or what a candidate could do if they disagreed.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> South Korea's AI Basic Act took effect on 22 January 2026<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Commentary describes core transparency duties for generative and high-impact AI, with high-impact uses, including areas such as hiring, bringing duties around risk management and human oversight<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Observers note the Act tries to balance innovation with social risk, and that its details are being filled in through implementing rules<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. With enforcement maturing and enterprise customers asking for assurances, the platform chose to build for the high-impact obligations now.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Every AI use known and classified</strong>, with hiring uses treated as high-impact.</li>
<li><strong>Candidates informed</strong> when AI is involved, with a route to human review.</li>
<li><strong>A person decides</strong>: AI can rank and summarise, but no rejection without a recruiter's decision.</li>
<li><strong>Models tested before release</strong>, including for differences across groups of candidates.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence on demand</strong> for any application: inputs, outputs, model version and decision.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>People</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Candidates<em>notices and review requests</em></span><span>Recruiters<em>decide in the workbench</em></span><span>Employers<em>assurance reports</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>AI services</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Matching and ranking<em>versioned models</em></span><span>Application summaries<em>LLM, cited to the CV</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Controls</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">AI inventory<em>use, risk class, owner</em></span><span class="hl">Evaluation gate<em>quality and fairness checks</em></span><span>Notice service<em>AI use disclosed</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Oversight</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Reviewer workbench<em>AI output beside the source</em></span><span>Decision log<em>who decided, and why</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>GenAI tracing<em>inputs, outputs, model</em></span><span>Model registry<em>versions and test results</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>AI assists; people decide; every step leaves evidence.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Apply</b><span>Candidate applies and sees a notice explaining where AI is used</span></li>
<li><b>Match</b><span>A versioned model ranks fit against the role's stated criteria</span></li>
<li><b>Summarise</b><span>An LLM drafts a summary that cites the CV for every claim</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Review</b><span>A recruiter sees AI output beside the source and decides</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Record</b><span>Decision, reviewer and model versions logged with the trace</span></li>
<li><b>Respond</b><span>Candidate can request human review or an explanation</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>The human decision is a required step in the flow, not an optional check.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: evidence without slowing recruiters down</h2>
<p>Oversight fails when it becomes paperwork. The platform made evidence a by-product of normal work: every AI call was traced with OpenTelemetry's GenAI conventions, capturing the input, the output, the tools used and the model, so any application could be reconstructed later<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The reviewer workbench put the AI summary next to the original CV with citations, so checking it took seconds rather than minutes.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/korea-ai-basic-act-hiring-platform/ai-step-trace.webp" alt="Trace detail for an AI agent call showing the user input message, the assistant output, the number of tool calls, duration and token count" width="1093" height="703" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>A traced AI step with its input, output and tool calls: the same record supports debugging, evaluation and accountability. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/genai-observability/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, James Newton-King</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<div class="callout"><p>In high-impact AI, the log is part of the product. If you cannot reconstruct a decision, you cannot defend it, improve it or explain it to the person it affected.</p></div>
<p>Personal data in traces was minimised and access-controlled; summaries were stored with the application, not in a separate analytics copy. Evaluation followed the approach in <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets that predict production</a>, with checks for differences across candidate groups added to the release gate. See also <a href="/insights/llm-observability-opentelemetry-genai/">LLM observability with OpenTelemetry</a>.</p>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Legal and policy</small><ol><li>Scope and risk classes</li><li class="hl">Notices and review rights</li><li>Employer contract terms</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Engineering and data</small><ol><li>AI inventory and registry</li><li class="hl">Evaluation gate and tracing</li><li>Reviewer workbench</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Recruitment operations</small><ol><li>Workbench design input</li><li>Decision guidelines</li><li class="hl">Handling review requests</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Recruiters shaped the workbench; that is why they used it.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Inventory</b><ul><li>Every AI use</li><li>Risk classification</li><li>Owners</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Evidence</b><ul><li>GenAI tracing</li><li>Model registry</li><li>Decision log</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Oversight</b><ul><li>Reviewer workbench</li><li>No automated rejections</li><li>Candidate notices</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Assure</b><ul><li>Evaluation gate</li><li>Review requests</li><li>Employer reporting</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Evidence before oversight: you cannot review what you did not record.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Treat all hiring AI as high-impact</td><td>Argue each use out of scope</td><td>Simpler, defensible, and what employers expected</td></tr>
<tr><td>No automated rejections</td><td>Auto-reject below a score</td><td>Keeps a person accountable for adverse decisions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Summaries must cite the CV</td><td>Free-text summaries</td><td>Reviewers can verify every claim quickly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tracing as the evidence store</td><td>Separate compliance logging</td><td>One record for debugging, evaluation and accountability</td></tr>
<tr><td>Group-level checks in the release gate</td><td>Periodic audits only</td><td>Problems found before candidates are affected</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: build the inventory and tracing first; everything else depended on them. Differently: design the candidate-facing review process earlier. Explaining an AI-assisted outcome in plain language was harder than producing the technical evidence. For broader governance context, see <a href="/case-studies/ai-governance-programme-eu-us-uae/">an AI governance programme across jurisdictions</a> and <a href="/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/">ISO 42001 vs NIST AI RMF</a>.</p>
<p>If you deploy AI in hiring, lending or other high-impact decisions in Asia-Pacific or elsewhere, my <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture work</a> and <a href="/regions/">regional pages</a> explain how I approach it, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
